<p>
  A workout you import is <strong>not published</strong>: its details and route
  stay private until you publish it. If you separately turn on statistics or
  record sharing on your profile, its figures can still contribute to those
  sections. No setting publishes for you unless you turn it on yourself.
</p>

<h2>A publication is a copy</h2>
<p>
  Publishing creates a <strong>publication</strong>: a copy of the workout,
  made at that moment, holding only what you chose to share. Your followers and
  the feed read that copy, never the workout itself. Because it is a copy:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>editing the workout afterwards — its title, description or sport — does
    not change the publication;</li>
  <li>to share an updated version, you <strong>replace the publication</strong>:
    the old one is deleted and a new one is created;</li>
  <li>your original file, your private notes and the points hidden by your
    privacy zones are never part of it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Publishing profiles</h2>
<p>
  A <strong>publishing profile</strong> is the recipe for that copy: which
  measures it carries (distance, time, elevation, speed, heart rate…), whether
  it shows the route, the map image, the GPX file, the device, the weather,
  your photos, whether comments are open, and whether the start and the finish
  are masked. Before you publish, the form lists exactly what will be shared and
  what will not.
</p>
<p>
  You get two built-in profiles, one per audience, and can create your own in
  <%= link_to "Publishing profiles", publishing_profiles_path %>. Changing a
  profile only affects future publications: existing ones keep the settings
  they were made with.
</p>
<p>
  Heart rate is health data: new profiles do not share it until you tick it.
</p>

<h2>Audiences</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Public</strong> — every member of this instance and, when this
    instance shares with others, the accounts that follow you there.</li>
  <li><strong>Followers</strong> — only the accounts that follow you, once you
    have accepted them. Those on this instance always see them; those on other
    instances receive them only if this instance's operator has enabled it.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  A workout can have one publication per audience: a detailed one for your
  followers and a lighter public one, for example. Each reader sees the version
  meant for them: your followers read the followers' one.
</p>

<h2>Waiting before it goes out</h2>
<p>
  A profile can hold publications back for a while — a day, three days, a week,
  two weeks or a month — so that, say, a run from home is not visible while you
  are still away. During the wait the publication is <strong>scheduled</strong>:
  nobody sees it, and you can publish it now, change the date or cancel it.
</p>

<h2>Automatic publishing</h2>
<p>
  If you mark a profile as automatic, each new workout is published with it as
  soon as its analysis is finished. It never applies to workouts older than
  seven days, to large imports (an archive, a batch of files), or, if you
  choose, to commutes or to the sports you exclude. You can decline a pending
  automatic publication from the workout page.
</p>

<h2>Deleting a publication</h2>
<p>
  Deleting a publication removes it from this instance and tells the other
  servers that have it to delete it too.
</p>
<div class="alert alert-info" role="note">
  <%= t("workouts.publication.confirm.body") %>
</div>
<p>
  Deleting the workout withdraws its publications the same way.
</p>

<h2>Statistics and records on your profile</h2>
<p>
  In <%= link_to t("settings.profile.title"), settings_profile_path %>, you can
  choose separately who can see your general statistics by sport and your
  personal records: your accepted followers or every member of this instance.
  Neither section is shared by default. Pending follow requests do not grant
  access to followers-only figures; unfollowing removes that access. Anonymous
  visitors never have access.
</p>
<p>
  These sections cover your whole workout history, across all years, even if the
  workouts are not published. They are independent of publishing profiles and
  journal filters; turning them on does not publish any workout.
</p>
<p>
  The statistics card's radar axes are the sports: choose the measure to show —
  workout count, distance, moving time or ascent. With fewer than three applicable
  sports, bars replace the radar.
</p>
<p><%= t("profile.statistics.log_scale") %></p>
<p>
  <%= t("profile.statistics.reading_hint") %>
  The tooltip shows the full sport name and the real value in your units, never
  the logarithm. Each axis end also lets you read zero values. A brief tap on
  mobile shows the same information without blocking swipes between measures
  or page scrolling. The bars offer the same reading, and the tables keep every
  value.
</p>
<div class="alert alert-info" role="note">
  <%= t("settings.profile.sharing.privacy") %>
</div>
